AB  - Seawater covers 96.5% of Earth’s water resources, making direct seawater electrolysis (DSE) a compelling pathway for sustainable hydrogen production, particularly when coupled with offshore renewable energy. Yet the complex ionic environment of seawater imposes formidable barriers: chloride-driven side reactions compete with oxygen evolution at the anode, magnesium and calcium precipitates foul cathode surfaces, and membrane materials degrade under sustained halide attack. Bridging the gap between laboratory promise and marine-scale deployment remains the central challenge of this field. This review traces the full technical chain from fundamental catalyst design to real-world offshore demonstration. At the anode, strategies including Lewis acid protective layers, anion-enriched surface engineering, and single-atom coordination modulation have pushed oxygen evolution selectivity beyond 99%, while cathode innovations such as phosphate passivation layers, bubble-assisted self-cleaning architectures, and dynamic pH regulation have enabled systems to withstand intermittent operation exceeding 10,000 h. At the membrane and electrolyzer level, asymmetric pH configurations, zero-gap cell designs, and bipolar membrane architectures collectively address chlorine suppression, scaling control, and mass transfer limitations. The emerging role of machine learning and density functional theory in accelerating catalyst screening and elucidating corrosion mechanisms is critically assessed, alongside the data scarcity bottlenecks that constrain model generalization to genuine seawater conditions. Field demonstrations, including megawatt-scale systems and the world’s first offshore wind-coupled desalination-free hydrogen platform, are examined to identify the engineering gaps that laboratory metrics consistently underestimate: biofouling, structural fatigue, gas crossover safety, and the absence of standardized evaluation protocols. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
