AB  - Photobioreactor technology for the treatment of gaseous streams while generating high-valueadded byproducts has gained significant relevance in recent years, offering an alternative for producing biodiesel, biogas, cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, and other products. These systems harness the photosynthetic capacities of different microalgal species by capturing CO2 and converting it into organic matter for growth. However, factors such as temperature, light intensity, nutrients, and pH affect the biomass production performance. Therefore, various photobioreactor operational control techniques have been developed to optimize microalgal production. This study presents the design, implementation, and performance evaluation of a fuzzy control system for regulating pH by injecting gaseous CO2 and assesses its impact on the growth rates of a culture of the microalga Chlorella vulgaris cf. applied to an airlift photobioreactor. The fuzzy control method was programmed using the free Arduino software and implemented on the Arduino Mega development board. Theoretical validation of the fuzzy control method was performed by analyzing the Arduino Mega output variables using the fuzzy logic toolbox simulation of MATLAB 2023R. Growth tests were conducted for 15 days in triplicate to guarantee the reproducibility of the study. The results demonstrate improved pH regulation (7.30 ± 0.05), showing an increase in productivity, with a maximum concentration of up to 400 ± 1.15 mg/L under control conditions. In comparison, tests conducted without CO2 injection control achieved concentrations below 150 ± 0.58 mg/L. Moreover, an increase in the maximum CO2 capture rate was observed from 18.44 ± 0.035 mgL-1d-1 to 65.85 ± 0.015 mgL-1d-1 for tests without and with pH control, respectively. These findings affirm that applying the fuzzy control method on pH presented in the present study improves the productivity of microalgae. © The Korean Institute of Intelligent Systems. This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution Non-Commercial License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0/).
